<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Baptism" cic="CCC 1210-1284" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does it mean to be <em>&laquo;born again&raquo;</em> according to Jesus in John 3:3-5?</li>
<li>Name two Old Testament events that prefigure Baptism and explain how each one points to the sacrament.</li>
<li>What are the matter and form of the sacrament of Baptism?</li>
<li>Who can be the minister of Baptism? Under what circumstances can someone other than a bishop, priest, or deacon baptize?</li>
<li>List the three principal effects of Baptism as taught in CCC 1262-1274.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no <em>&laquo;correct&raquo;</em> answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Do you know the date and place of your own Baptism? Who were your godparents?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">If you don&rsquo;t know, ask your parents or look for your baptismal certificate. Write what you discover.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Baptism makes you a child of God, a member of the Church, and a temple of the Holy Spirit. Which of these three truths do you find hardest to believe about yourself? Why?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Is there a sin or habit you need to <em>&laquo;drown&raquo;</em> in the waters of God&rsquo;s mercy? What would it mean to let Baptism have its full effect in your life?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. How can you live out your baptismal promises this week: to renounce sin and to follow Christ?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Choose one concrete action and write it down.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. The Catechism teaches that Baptism leaves an indelible mark on the soul. What does this say about God&rsquo;s faithfulness to you, even when you fail?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Find your baptismal record</h4>
<p>Ask your parents, contact your parish, or look for your baptismal certificate. Write down the date, the church, your godparents&rsquo; names, and the priest who baptized you. If you can, light a candle on the anniversary of your Baptism as a reminder.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Read Romans 6:3-4 slowly</h4>
<p>St. Paul says we were buried with Christ in Baptism and raised to walk in newness of life. Write a short paragraph about what <em>&laquo;newness of life&raquo;</em> means to you right now.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Renew your baptismal promises</h4>
<p>Find the renewal of baptismal promises in a missal or online. Pray them slowly, paying attention to each renunciation and each profession of faith. Afterward, write which promise struck you most and why.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p><em>&laquo;Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you.&raquo;</em></p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Mt 28:19-20</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, who sanctified the waters of the Jordan<br />
and commanded your Church to baptize all nations,<br />
renew in me the grace of my Baptism.<br />
I was born again of water and the Spirit;<br />
help me to live as a child of God,<br />
to renounce sin and to follow you faithfully.<br />
May I never forget the dignity you have given me<br />
and may my life reflect the newness of life<br />
that began in the waters of Baptism.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

